  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAs of 2 AM Saturday morning, the midlevel pattern remains unchanged \nfrom Friday with midlevel ridging across the eastern 2/3rds of the \nCONUS and trough near the Pacific coast. A weak surface trough \ncontinues to reside across the high Plains. Within this trough axis \na weak midlevel MCV continues to very slowly push across southwest \nKS. This is yielding showers and thunderstorms southwest of DDC. \nDecreasing instability and increasing convective inhibition across \ncentral and south-central KS should keep conditions dry this \nmorning. Transitioning into the afternoon hours, another hot and \nmostly dry is expected with high temperatures near 100 once again. \nSteepening low-level lapse rates will most convective inhibition \nearly this afternoon, especially across central KS. The midlevel \nridge axis will setup across central KS, likely limiting any \nthunderstorm coverage to very isolated, if any at all. Further west, \nthunderstorm development is expected late this afternoon within the \nsurface trough axis across the high Plains. These thunderstorms will \nslowly move east into the early morning hours Sunday. As the LLJ \nveers near dawn Sunday morning, a few thunderstorms cannot be ruled \nout across central KS. Additionally, a complex of thunderstorms may \ndevelop within a low/midlevel baroclinic zone across eastern SD and \nMN late tonight. This complex may gradually move south, within this \nweak baroclinic zone. Considerable uncertainty exists with the \nsouthward extent this complex may propagate but it stands watching \nnonetheless. By Sunday afternoon, the aforementioned low-level \nbaroclinic zone will remain across eastern KS. PW values will \napproach 1.75\" across eastern and may yield a few airmass \nthunderstorms. Widespread thunderstorms are not expected, \nunfortunately. A similar scenario may play out Monday afternoon \nacross eastern KS. \n\nMid to long range guidance suggests the midlevel ridge may briefly \nweaken during the middle and second half of next week as a shortwave \ntrough ejects across the northern states. This may push a cold front \ntowards central and southern KS with the possibility of cooler \ntemperatures and rain chances.",
